Drafted by WORKER-4, round 2 (W4-S2). NOT APPLIED — .orca/*.md is the
ORCHESTRATOR's ground per WORKER.md. Apply this to .orca/RAILWAY.md if agreed.

Why: the "Verify - by artefact, not by the green light" step curls a fixed
path (`/assets/michael.js`) on a fixed domain
(`michael-hermes-production.up.railway.app`) and diffs its sha256 against
`git show <target-commit>:web/michael.js`. Both assumptions are now wrong,
confirmed live (read-only GET/HEAD, no mutation):

  $ curl -sI https://michael-hermes-production.up.railway.app/
  HTTP/1.1 404 Not Found
  x-railway-fallback: true
  $ curl -s  https://michael-hermes-production.up.railway.app/api/health
  {"status":"error","code":404,"message":"Application not found", ...}

That domain no longer resolves to any deployed service at all — consistent
with docs/gate-cutover.md Step 6/7 (remove the michael-hermes public domain
once michael-gate takes over) having already run. Separately, even before
that: commit 0c98f30 "feat(gate): serve the chat page from the gate, not the
Hermes image" removed the COPY that put web/michael.html and web/michael.js
into the Hermes image at all, and the era before that hashed the served JS
filename (evidenced in .orca/reports/2026-09-22-wave3-rollback-verification.md:
`/assets/michael-c6cc4e57dcc6.js`, 200; `/assets/michael.js`, 404 "because the
built asset is content-hashed"). So the literal `/assets/michael.js` path was
already wrong for any hermes rollback target after the hashing fix, and is
doubly wrong now that hermes serves no web assets at all.

The fix below does three things: (1) stop assuming a fixed public domain —
read it from `railway status`/`railway domain` for the service actually being
rolled back; (2) branch on whether the rollback target predates the gate
cutover (0c98f30), since only pre-cutover hermes commits serve the web assets
at all, and those serve them under a content-hash the doc must read from the
served HTML rather than assume; (3) for a michael-gate rollback, verify against
the gate's own fixed, unhashed `/michael.js` path instead.

--- a/.orca/RAILWAY.md
+++ b/.orca/RAILWAY.md
@@ -169,17 +169,42 @@
 ### 3. Verify - by artefact, not by the green light

 A rollback that only checks "is the service Online" repeats this project's
 own mistake of trusting a status light over a measurement (see
 `.orca/PRODUCTION-READY.md`'s own framing, and W4-7). After the rollback
 reports success:

 ```
-railway status                                                    # new deployment ID matches the target
-curl -sI https://michael-hermes-production.up.railway.app/assets/michael.html
-curl -sI https://michael-hermes-production.up.railway.app/assets/michael.js
+railway status                                                    # new deployment ID matches the target
+railway domain --service <the service being rolled back>          # the CURRENT public domain, not a remembered one
 ```

-Compare the served bytes' sha256 (LF-normalised - see finding W4-1) against
-`git show <target-commit>:web/michael.html` / `:web/michael.js` from this
-repo at the commit identified in step 1. A matching hash is the proof the
-rollback actually landed the intended build, not merely that Railway reports
-a green status - the same distinction D1-D3 already draw for a forward
-deploy. Re-check `.orca/ro.sh cli hosts` (or another cheap read) to confirm
-the container is actually serving, not just reporting `Online` while still
-booting.
+**Do not assume `michael-hermes-production.up.railway.app` still resolves.**
+Since commit 0c98f30 ("serve the chat page from the gate, not the Hermes
+image"), `michael-hermes` no longer serves `web/michael.html` or
+`web/michael.js` at all - `michael-gate` does - and per
+docs/gate-cutover.md Step 6/7 the old public domain on `michael-hermes` is
+removed once the gate takes over. Confirm the actual domain for the service
+you rolled back with `railway domain` before curling anything.
+
+**Rolling back `michael-gate`:** it serves the asset unhashed at a fixed
+path. Compare directly:
+
+```
+curl -s https://<the gate's current domain>/michael.js | sha256sum
+git show <target-commit>:web/michael.js | sha256sum
+```
+
+(LF-normalise if they differ only in line endings - see finding W4-1.) A
+match is the proof the rollback landed the intended build, not merely that
+Railway reports a green status.
+
+**Rolling back `michael-hermes` to a commit before 0c98f30:** that era
+served the page itself, and a separate era within it (see
+.orca/reports/2026-09-22-wave3-rollback-verification.md) content-hashed the
+served JS filename, so `/assets/michael.js` may 404 even on a correct
+rollback. Read the actual filename out of the served HTML instead of
+assuming one:
+
+```
+curl -s https://<hermes's current domain>/assets/michael.html \
+  | grep -o 'michael-[0-9a-f]\{12\}\.js' | head -1
+curl -s https://<hermes's current domain>/assets/<name-from-above> | sha256sum
+git show <target-commit>:web/michael.js | sha256sum
+```
+
+**Rolling back `michael-hermes` to a commit at or after 0c98f30:** it no
+longer serves either web asset, so this artefact check does not apply to it.
+Verify instead against something hermes still serves for that commit, e.g.
+`.orca/ro.sh cli hosts` succeeding (the container is actually up and the
+MCP path is live), and/or the `/api/health` `version` field if that commit
+changed it.
